police officer eric talley was one of 10  people killed in last week's supermarket  mass shooting  in boulder yesterday his friends and  family gathered to say  goodbye 500 law enforcement vehicles  from across the country  participated in a procession escorting  his hearse wow look at that  to the memorial service just outside of  boulder janet shamley and watched the  tributes  the cars stretched for some eight miles  a number of first responders on  horseback  and people gathered on the side of the  road in below freezing temperatures  as a community came together to say  goodbye to officer eric  talley i just feel it's so important to  let the family know that we're here and  we're supporting them and we care  at the church fellow officers saluted  the flag draped casket  and family photos of the 51 year old  husband and father of  seven were shared as his life  and heroism were honored your father was  kind  your father died a hero there is no  doubt  because of his bravery dozens of  innocent lives were saved tally was the  first officer to arrive  at the king superstore when a gunman  opened fire last week  nine others also died police say talley  was the last to be shot  in that rampage his sergeant recalled  talley's commitment  eric was the kind of guy who was so  vibrant and enthusiastic about life and  the job  he wanted to share with the world he was  the kind of person to give you the shirt  off his back  even if he had to borrow it from someone  else first eric  would give his all and give his all he  did  many now hoping officer talley's  sacrifice will be remembered  not by his death but by how he lived  eric talley's patrol car parked here  outside the boulder police department  now covered in flowers and tributes his  family asked  not to appear on camera during the  service but they did offer a poem that  his seven children  wrote for him and gave to him on  christmas in 2019  and it reads in part dad our unsung hero  you daily risk your life to guard and  care for the welfare of the needy  oh our praises could never be enough  they sound like pretty great kids tony  janet thank you pretty great kids but  pretty great dad pretty great dad pretty  great family and then eric talley's  father did an interview a couple of days  ago where he said  his son was always known for doing the  right thing and he died doing the right  thing  it's tough looking at the family photos  though of what was lost that day it  really is and he didn't have to be a  police officer he chose to do that  late in life their journey is just  beginning that family and i hope that i  wish them the best we all do
 

